       Personal Accounts Manager (PAM) - User Guide for V1.1a


Introduction
------------
PAM is a Personal Accounts Manager program for the Organiser 3. This
version is an Alpha test version and some of the functions are not
implemented. Those functions that are implemented have been tested
over a period of time and are thought to be bug free and reliable.
However, the author accepts no responsibility for any financial mis-
management or losses resulting from the use of this program.   This
release of the program is Public domain,  it may be freely copied or
distributed providing the following conditions are met:

  1)  In may not be sold or traded for financial or personal gain.
     
  2)  It must be copied complete including this document.
     
  3)  Its use is restricted to personal use,  it may not be used by
      commercial organisations,  businesses, corporations or public
      authorities.
     
  4)  The program code may not be modified in anyway.
     

History
-------
The Personal Account Manager was originally developed for the
Organiser II,  the original 2 line version.    This was adopted by a
number of friends and colleagues and much of the current
functionality was developed as a result of their suggestions.   The
program was enhanced to utilise the improved display of the 4 line
versions of the Organiser II.   This version while retaining the core
of the earlier versions is virtually a total re-write to utilise the
very different user interface of the Series 3 and its ability to
multi-task.   This version has greatly improved account review and
modify facilities.


Installation
------------
The LHA archive file,  self extracting,  contains three files;  this
document,  the program itself (PAM.OPA) and the Icon (PAM.PIC).
These should be copied into the \APP\ directory on the Organiser.
The application should then be installed from the System screen using
Install application.   When first used you will be prompted to
configure the account settings,  see System - Config for details of
account types and how to declare them.   At least one account must be
configured to use the program.   Subsequently,  new accounts may be
added or existing accounts re-configured without disturbing existing
accounts,  using the System - Config option.


Overview
--------
PAM supports up to 12 separate accounts.   Options are provided to
credit monies to an account,  debit monies from an account,  transfer
monies between accounts,  review the current and predicted month end
balance for an account.   Review the current transactions entered for
an account. Review the expired transactions for an account.   Modify
or erase existing transactions with an option to include the value of
erased transactions in either the running or temporary balances for
the account.   It will also automatically update accounts for monthly
standing orders.


System considerations
---------------------
PAM is written in OPL and while the user interface has been written
to match that of the standard applications the limitations of OPL
mean that certain functions could not be implemented.   Primarily the
Help key only works from the result space display.   PAM is a type 0
application and can be left running permanently providing you are not
short of system memory.   PAM conforms to the type 0 rules and will
not inhibit auto switch off and can be closed from the System menu
providing no menu or dialogue boxes are displayed within PAM itself.
PAM itself requires 10K plus room for the accounts files.   The
automatic update of Standing Order transactions occurs as soon as an
Event is passed into the PAM window after the day has changed.   If
PAM is the foreground window this update will occur shortly after
midnight.   If PAM is a background window this will occur as soon as
PAM is called to the foreground the update takes about 5 seconds.
PAM creates two data files the main one holds the current
transactions and is permanently open when PAM is running.   The peak
size will depend on the maximum number of transactions entered.
Since the majority of transactions will be deleted once an account
statement is received this file will cycle in size between fairly
well defined maximum and minimum sizes,  probably 2-8K.   The expired
transaction account however,  will continue to grow slowly but
steadily and requires manual intervention to clear it at regular
intervals.


Operation
---------
The following section describes each of the available transaction
option.   These may be accessed either by pressing <Menu> then
selecting the required option using the cursor movement keys or
directly from the results display screen by using the 'accelerator
key' option i.e.; Psion+<letter>.   Throughout this document Ò is
used to indicate the Psion key.

Unless otherwise stated the <Esc> key may be used to abort an
operation at any time before it is completed.   While the menu or any
dialogue box is displayed the automatic update is inhibited even if
the PAM window is left in foreground.   To reduce the chances of this
happening when using options which pause while presenting long
duration displays i.e. when the 'Press a Key' message is showing on
the top right of the screen,  the listing will restart automatically
after 5 minutes.

PAM may be left in background or closed for prolonged periods i.e.
months,  without losing its ability to track transactions correctly
however in may take some time to complete the automatic update when
PAM is first selected under these conditions.   While this will not
cause any loss of data it may mean that the automatic update has to
make up for several days without update and this will extend the time


Review - Account - ÒR
---------------------
This option will list all the active transactions currently entered
for an account.   When selected you will be prompted to select an
account and once this has been selected and <Enter> pressed the first
seven transactions will be listed.   If there are more than seven
transactions for the account a message 'Press a Key' will flash on
the top right of the screen.   Pressing any key other than <Esc> will
cause the next seven transactions to be listed.   This will repeat
until all transaction for the account have been listed.   Each line
of the display is a single transaction which is in the following
format:

    nn.nn dd/mm/yy type ac1 [di]  [ac2]  description
    |     |        |    |   |     |      |
    |     |        |    |   |     |      Description of transaction as
    |     |        |    |   |     |      entered in the notes field.
    |     |        |    |   |     |
    |     |        |    |   |     Second account name, first 3 letters 
    |     |        |    |   |     off, only present for transfer transactions.
    |     |        |    |   |
    |     |        |    |   Direction of flow of monies,  indicates which account is  the
    |     |        |    |   source and which the destination,  only present for transfer
    |     |        |    |   transactions.
    |     |        |    |
    |     |        |    First account name,  first 3 letters off, always present.
    |     |        |
    |     |        The transaction type ,  first 3 letters off, will be followed by the number of
    |     |        months remaining for standing orders with a finite life.
    |     |
    |     Date  for which the transaction was recorded.
    |
    Transaction amount, +ve value for credits,  -ve values for debits.
     

Review - Archive - ÒA
---------------------
This option will list all expired transactions for a selected
account.   Operation is the same as Review - Account, as is the
display format.


Review - Balance - ÒB
---------------------
You will be prompted to select which account.   Once selected the
account balances are calculated based on the current transactions.
Four balances will be displayed:

     Current   The account balance at the current date.
               
     Month end The estimated month end balance based on the currently
               entered transactions for the account.
               
     Temporary The current value of the temporary balance entry.
               
     Running   The current value of the running balance record.
               

Review - Overall - ÒO
---------------------
This option presents a quick summary of the current and month end
balance for all accounts in the format:

     Cu(rrent) Amount    Mn(Month end)  Value     Account name.
     
In the current implementation this option only works correctly for
eight accounts or less as the scroll control has not been implement
for this option.


System - About - ÒI
-------------------
This will display the program name,  version,  date and other
information.   This display is shown automatically the first time the
program is initiated i.e. until the accounts data file has been
created.


System - Config - ÒF
--------------------
The configure option is used to declare accounts.   Up twelve
accounts may be declared and the accounts may be either Current or
Credit Account types:

The accounts may be declared as either Credit or Current.   While
this has not effect on the way they are processed it does alter what
types of transaction are allowed.

     Credit Accounts
     ---------------
     A credit account is the simpler of the two account types.   It
     is primarily intended for Credit / Charge cards.   Transactions
     originating in a credit account can only be of one type i.e.
     credit card.
     
     Current Accounts
     ----------------
     A current account is primarily intended for standard current
     accounts.   It supports four transaction types;  Cheque,  ATM
     card,  Bank giro and Standing order.   Of these the first three
     are convenience tags and have no other effects.   Standing order
     is special in that this tag enables the automatic monthly update
     of a transaction.   A Standing order will be processed on the
     given date and it's date shifted on one month until either it
     expires or is cancelled.
     

  Notes on Naming
  ---------------
  The account name may be up to eight characters but the following
  should be considered when picking account names.   The first
  letter of the account name can be used to select the account in
  the dialogue boxes so each account will be easier to select if it
  starts with a separate letter.   When display space is tight only
  the first three letters of the account name is displayed so if
  possible the first three letters should be meaningful.   Any
  character may be used as part of the account name.
  
  Notes on re-configuring accounts
  --------------------------------
  Internally the accounts are identified by a number key based on
  their position in the configuration table.   This means that re-
  naming an account will rename all transaction associated with that
  key this includes both those in the current transaction file and
  those in the expired transaction file.   For this reason it is not
  possible to move the position of an existing account within the
  configuration table.
  

System - Exit - ÒX
------------------
This option will close the PAM program and associated data files.
This will reduce the memory requirement by about 10k.   PAM may also
be closed from the System menu using the delete key,  provided no
menu or dialogue windows are open within PAM.   You must close PAM
before your try to access the Pam.odb file with any other program.
The Pamx.odb (archive file) is open only during automatic update and
modify transaction operations.   You do not need to close PAM to
access this file.


System - Export - ÒP
--------------------
This function is not currently implemented.   It was intended that
this function allow the expired transaction file to be down loaded
via the Comms link to a PC or printer then clear the expired
transaction file.   The question is in what format?   Currently
Pamx.odb my be exported using the copy command via the Comms link.
The Pamx.odb file may be deleted with the System File Delete command
when it grows to large.


System - Status (on/off) - ÒS
-----------------------------
This option turns on and off the status window on the right-hand side
of the display.   Provided for compatibility with in-built
applications.   Leaving it off has the advantage of leaving a larger
display area.   The current version of the OS 1.77F allows the status
window to be popped up for about 5 seconds pressing Ò<Menu>.


Transaction - Credit - ÒC
-------------------------
The credit option is identical in operation to the Transaction  -
Debit,  exception the entered amount is credit to and not debited
from the selected account.   It  is entered in the same way.


Transaction - Debit - ÒD
------------------------
When selected you will be asked which account.   Select an account
either using the left / right cursor keys or the first letter of the
account name.   You can view the account names in list form by
pressing <Tab>.   Once the correct account is selected press <Enter>.

If it is a credit account you will go directly to the transaction
detail screen.   If it is a current account you will be prompt to
select one of the four transaction types.   Once this has been
selected you will be presented with the transaction screen.

To move between lines on the transaction screen use the up / down
cursor keys.   Do not press <Enter> until you have completed all the
lines of the screen.   To abandon an entry press <Esc>.   A
transaction must have a none zero value to be recorded.

  Note:  This is a free format text field to describe the
         transaction.   You may enter up to 80 characters but as
         each character uses memory and the cumulative effect can be
         significant so these descriptions should be as short as
         would be meaningful to you.
          
  Date:  The date is initially set to the current date by default
         but may be amended to match the actual date of the
         transaction either in the past or future.
          
  Amount:This is the transaction value which must be entered as
         positive value.   A zero value will cause the transaction
         to be discarded.
          
  Months:This line is present only if the transaction is a Standing
         order.   The number of months may be set to any value
         between 0 and 120 (5 years).   The standing order will then
         be processed until the specified number of months have
         passed then expire.   The 0 months is a special case in
         that it will continue until it is cancelled manually.
          

Transaction - Modify - ÒM
-------------------------
This option allows you to view each transaction for an account and if
required modify or erase it.   When selected you will be prompted to
select an account once selected the transaction detail screen is
presented with the cursor on the Action line.   One  of the following
actions may the be selected:

  Next:  When select and <Enter> is press.   The displayed
         transaction closed unchanged and the next transaction for
         the account displayed.   This is the default action and
         allows you to step through all the current transaction in
         the account file.
          
  Erase: This option will cause the displayed transaction to be
         erased when <Enter> is pressed.   The next transaction is
         then displayed.   If you try to erase a Standing order you
         will be asked to confirm the erasure.   The value of erased
         transactions is summed.
         
  Modify:This option allows the existing entry to be modified.
         While you may select and modify any of the displayed
         details these changes are only saved if Modify is selected
         before <Enter> is pressed.   You may only modify a single
         transaction on each pass of Transaction - Modify.   The
         value of a modified transaction is not summed.
          
If when the last transaction has been viewed the sum of the erased
total is not zero you will be prompted as to what to do with this
erased total:

  None - <Esc> Selecting None causes the erased total to be
               discarded.
                
  Tmp: - <Tab> Selecting TeMPorary causes the erased total to be
               saved as a temporary balance for the account.   This
               temporary balance is utilised by the Transaction -
               Transfer option.   This option is normally used for
               Credit accounts.
                
  Run: - <Enter>   Selecting Running balance causes the erased total
                   to be summed into the accounts running balance.   This
                   option is normally used for Current accounts.
               
Each transactions added to the account file is displayed as a single
line summary on the background screen.   The format of this line is
the same as the review options.   Each transaction deleted from the
account file is displayed as single line summary struck through by a
diagonal line.   Remember that if you erase or modify a transfer
transaction you affect only the entry in the selected account.   The
matching half in the other account needs to be erased or modified
separately.


Transaction - Transfer - ÒT
---------------------------
This option is basically the same as a debit from the source account
and credit to the destination account.   You will prompted for the
name of the source account and the transaction type.   You will then
be prompted for the destination account,  this cannot be the same as
the source account.   Once the destination account has been selected
you will be presented with the transaction screen.   The entries on
this are the same as for credit and debit with one exception.   If
the destination account contains a temporary balance entry, see
Transaction - Modify,  the value of this balance will be entered in
the amount field.   This value may be overwritten with a new value is
required.

This option will actually generate two entries in the data file,  one
debit and one credit,  this is to allow the two halves of the
transfer to be erased separately but if you Modify,  see Transaction
- Modify,  a transfer you must remember to Modify both entries.


Notes on result display
-----------------------
The result display is the main program window in PAM and forms the
backdrop to all other displays.   Unless PAM is exited this display
will contain either the results of your last request or the result
from the last automatic update which occurred.   To ensure that PAM
updates at the soonest possible moment it is recommended that you
return to the results display by completing any option before
switching off your Organiser or leaving it for a prolonged period.
You may switch PAM into background into background at any time e.g.
to use the Calculator.


Standing Orders
---------------
While a transaction type is tagged as Standing order this is to some
extent a convenience name.   The primary function of this tag is to
enable the automatic processing of transactions which occur on a
given day each month.   This option may therefore be used for direct
debits,  for constant amounts,  or any other transaction of this
type.   It can also be used for a one off payment at a future date
which want to be deleted automatically from the current transactions
list.


The way forward
---------------
While I find the program meet my personal most of my personal
requirements I am always open to suggestions for enhancements.   A
few that I am currently considering is the inclusion in the
Transaction - Modify of basic calculator functions and the ability to
export (or import) selected totals to (from) the Calculator memories.
Inclusion of the ability to estimate interest on interest yielding
current accounts.   Also the ability to calculated the interest
payable on Credit accounts and the inclusion of a warning about the
need to erase / modify the partner of a transfer transactions or
possibly the option to amend it automatically after confirmation.

